.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt SIDECAR 1
.Os ELSEWHERE
.Sh NAME
.Nm sidecar
.Nd check a byte-range sidecar and select the bytes it describes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar file
.Nm
.Cm select
.Ar file
.Ar content
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads one sidecar in the
.Ql weft-byte-range/1
format of
.Xr sidecar 5 .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm check Ar file
Check that
.Ar file
follows the format: its header grammar, its key table, every value's
alphabet and its size limits, and that its body is UTF-8 text.
Print nothing and exit 0 when it does.
.It Cm select Ar file content
Check
.Ar file
as above, then resolve it against
.Ar content ,
a regular file the caller names as the place to look.
The name grants nothing: the bytes are accepted only by their size and
digest.
.Nm
reads the whole of
.Ar content
once into memory, verifies that its size equals
.Cm content_size
and its SHA256 equals
.Cm content_sha256 ,
and then writes the selected range, from
.Cm offset
for
.Cm length
bytes, to standard output from that verified copy.
Nothing is written before the content is verified.
.El
.Pp
This is the content-only resolver profile.
It has no store, index or network source: the caller names the content
file.
Its capacity is 67108864 bytes of content; a larger
.Cm content_size
is refused before anything is read.
It does not verify native provenance, so a sidecar that carries
.Cm docid
and
.Cm revision_sha256
passes
.Cm check
but is refused by
.Cm select ,
never resolved as content-only.
.Pp
Each refusal is one line on standard error naming the file, the header line
when there is one, and the reason.
.Nm
stops at the first refusal; there is no partial result and no repair.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is the public result.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success.
For
.Cm select ,
standard output holds exactly the selected bytes, which may be none.
.It 1
The description is malformed or unsupported: it breaks the grammar, a key
table rule, an alphabet or a size limit of
.Xr sidecar 5 ,
names another schema, or asks
.Cm select
to verify native provenance.
.It 2
The sidecar or the content is unavailable: missing, not readable, or not a
regular file.
.It 3
The content's identity does not match: its size or its digest differs from
the description, including when the file changes while it is read.
.It 4
The range lies outside the content, or
.Cm content_size
is over this profile's capacity, or memory for the content cannot be
allocated.
.It 5
Reading the input or writing standard output failed.
.It 6
Usage or setup failure: wrong operands, or
.Xr pledge 2
refused.
.El
.Pp
After any status but 0, discard whatever standard output holds: a write
failure can leave part of the range behind.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Describe the last two bytes of the three-byte content
.Ql abc ,
check the description, and select the bytes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf abc > abc
$ cat > abc.md <<'EOF'
---
schema: "weft-byte-range/1"
content_sha256: "ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad"
content_size: "3"
offset: "1"
length: "2"
---
# A small passage
EOF
$ sidecar check abc.md
$ sidecar select abc.md abc
bc
.Ed
.Pp
The selected bytes have no newline, so the shell prompt follows them on the
same line.
After the content changes, the same description is refused:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf abX > abc
$ sidecar select abc.md abc
sidecar: abc: digest differs from content_sha256
$ echo $?
3
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sha256 1 ,
.Xr sidecar 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The format and the result classes follow
.Pa policy/ADDRESSING.md ,
edition
.Ql 2026-10-13.v3 .
.Sh AUTHORS
Written by Claude (claude.ai/code) for ELSEWHERE, card WARP-PORT, from the
Python sidecar validator and warp object store of src.git 75ad5ce4.
.Sh CAVEATS
A digest identifies bytes.
It does not establish authorship, truth, consent or access, and a sidecar
grants no authority to fetch or publish anything.
.Pp
The whole content is held in memory while it is verified, up to the
capacity above.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Nm
protects its caller from serving bytes other than the ones described: it
never clamps a range, never serves unverified or substitute bytes, and never
reports success without a verified selection.
It trusts the kernel, the SHA256 implementation of the C library, and the
caller's choice of operand paths.
Before reading any byte, it opens its operands and then calls
.Xr pledge 2
with
.Qq stdio
alone, so it cannot open, create or execute anything, use the network or
touch any other path; it may only read the two descriptors it holds and
write standard output and standard error.
.Xr unveil 2
is not used, because no path operation remains after that point.
Nothing is delegated and nothing persists; a failure leaves no state.
